course: dictionary
lesson: 5470
title: Mostly, but not all
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The word largely means mostly or mainly. It tells you that something is true to a great extent, but not completely. «Hunters such as cats and owls have eyes at the front, so their two eyes see largely the same view.» Their eyes see almost the same thing, but not exactly the same, so largely means mostly.
explain: Look for the clue. «The problem was largely caused by» one main thing, but the word largely warns you that the cause is not the whole story. If the sentence said all, there would be no room for any exceptions at all.
explain: Try the swap test. Put all in place of largely and ask whether the sentence still makes sense. «Wild orangutans are critically endangered, largely because forests were cut down for palm oil farms.» Forest loss is the main reason, so largely fits. Saying all would claim too much.
fact: Largely means mostly or mainly, but not completely.
fact: All means every single one; largely leaves room for a few exceptions.
fact: Phrases you may meet are largely because, largely due to, largely made of and largely ignored.
fact: «Russia expanded east across Siberia from the 1580s and reached the Pacific coast by the 1640s, largely in search of furs.» Furs were the main reason for the journey east.
fact: In maths, largely can describe most of a group without giving an exact number.
exercise: «The desert is largely sand.» What does largely mean here? | *Mostly, though not all | Only a tiny bit of it is sand | Every single grain of it is sand | Not sand at all
exercise: «The Suez Canal was dug largely by Egyptian workers.» What does largely show about the Egyptian workers? | They dug only at night | *Most of the digging was done by them | All of the digging was done by them | None of the digging was done by them
exercise: Which word is closest in meaning to largely? | never | barely | *mainly | completely
test: Ella says largely means all. Is she right? | Yes, largely means every single one | *No, largely means mostly, not all | No, largely means very loud | Yes, largely means none at all
test: «Hunters such as cats and owls have eyes at the front, so their two eyes see largely the same view.» What does largely tell you? | Owls cannot see at all | *Their eyes see almost the same view, but not exactly | Their eyes never see the same view | Their eyes see completely different views
exam: Which sentence uses largely correctly? | The forest was largely quick and loud | *Most of the forest was cut down for farms, though a few trees still stand | Forests are largely the colour of the sea | The forest was entirely cut down, every single tree gone
